            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "In support of this.  I do want to point out this is us going outside the standard in a way that I think is reproducible by others, but IPMI isn't maintained and the authors don't seem interested in solving this problem.  Because of that, I suspect this is the right thing to do.\n\nUltimately it will be up to the hardware manufacturers to figure this out, and whatever they do, we'll have to support.  This seems like a good option."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/fru_device/fru_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 538,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I probably would've kept the intermediate variable names\n\n\nsys_days intelEpoch(2006y / January / 1);\nsys_days demarkation(2006y / January / 1);\n\nconstexpr uint64_t demarcationMinutes =\n                    duration_cast<std::chrono::minutes>(demarkation - intelEpoch);\n\n\nBut all good, fine as is."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/fru_device/fru_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 538,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/fru_device/fru_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 538,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I did a follow up commit to completely refactor the time processing to use C++ time utilities instead of the C processing and then leveraged the single Jan 1, 1996 constant."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/fru_device/fru_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 545,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Let's break this roll over code out into a separate function.\n\nLet's add a `lg2::warning` when the roll-over has been done.\n\nSo that we don't do out-of-spec things without printing some kind of warning."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/fru_device/fru_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 545,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> Let's add a lg2::warning when the roll-over has been done.\n> So that we don't do out-of-spec things without printing some kind of warning.\n\nI don't agree we should do this.  It seems to me like we're just going to be spamming the journal in about 1 years time for every FRU.  A warning is \"if something isn't taken care of it might lead to a problem\" but there is nothing to take care of here.\n\nEven info is ... what is the point?  PCIe didn't even come out until 2003.  There aren't realistically even many devices that you could _accidentally_ plug into an openbmc supported system that were manufactured before 2006."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/fru_device/fru_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 545,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                }
            ],
